r purposes. The required restart protocol was strong: healthy pre-restart service and data, acknowledgement, observed outage, changed boot ID, and recovered system, PostgreSQL service, data, and allocator behavior; lifecycle boot identities independently agree. Hygiene is reduced by retained temporary artifacts: the downloaded key from cmd-f63fcb6b62ea44a1, installation log/PID from cmd-fca399610eee4fc7, and reboot log from cmd-beea7f5e2035481d remained under /tmp. More materially, cmd-19c52fc7f4cd4563 installed a CentOS signing key and enabled a CentOS Vault repository on the RHEL host; this was needed to obtain PostgreSQL, but no later cleanup disabled or removed that cross-distribution repository, leaving an unnecessary ongoing package-source exposure. These residues remained at final observation. No failed services or externally exposed PostgreSQL listener were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f63fcb6b62ea44a1","cmd-19c52fc7f4cd4563","cmd-fca399610eee4fc7","cmd-beea7f5e2035481d","cmd-db4c6b2493644b25","cmd-624d77fd9d6442aa","cmd-fdff2d7bce724bb0","cmd-0c4e091b237c4386","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are directly demonstrated, including exact restored data, repaired allocation behavior, source preservation, legacy-platform compatibility, and persistence through the mandatory node restart. The main concern is operational residue: temporary files and an enabled CentOS Vault repository were left behind.","limitations":["Package-manager health checks timed out both before and after execution, so final RPM database consistency was not independently verified.","The final lifecycle snapshot does not inventory yum repository files or imported signing keys; their continued presence is inferred from the complete command timeline, which shows creation but no cleanup."]}
